// draft
// drafted
type Arrangement = { items: Text[] }

derangements(iterable: Text[], r: Option<Int>) -> Arrangement[]
	note "yield every arrangement of r of the iterable's elements, or of all of them when r is not given, in which no element sits at its original index, with elements told apart by position and not by value"
	# Every derangement has r items when r is given, and as many items as the iterable when it is
	# not.
	all d in out: (r == None -> len(d.items) == len(iterable)) and (r != None -> len(d.items) == r)
	# Every derangement's items are drawn from the iterable: each item is an occurrence of an input
	# element, and no occurrence is used twice.
	all d in out: d.items drawn_from iterable
	# No derangement places an input occurrence at its own index: the item at each index of a
	# derangement comes from a different index of the iterable, even when the two have the same
	# value.
	// reaching for: for each derangement, a one-to-one assignment of input positions to its items
	// in which no item at index i came from input position i. A derangement is a list of Text and
	// does not record which input position each item came from. The notation has no binder over
	// indices and no existential over assignments. So it can only compare values, and that would
	// wrongly reject ["Bob", "Alice", "Bob"] from ["Alice", "Bob", "Bob"].
	# Every arrangement of the right length drawn from the iterable that leaves no occurrence at its
	# own index is in the output.
	// reaching for: a quantifier over every arrangement of r input positions (or all of them when r
	// is None) with no position at its own index, saying the arrangement of values it picks out is
	// in out. The notation quantifies only over lists that exist in the inputs or out, not over all
	// permutations of a list.
	# Each arrangement of input positions appears in the output exactly once, so two derangements
	# may be equal by value when the iterable repeats a value.
	// reaching for: the multiplicity of each value arrangement in out equals the number of
	// fixed-point-free arrangements of input positions that produce it. This needs each
	// derangement's source positions, which the output does not carry, and a count over position
	// arrangements. `unique` and `drawn_from` compare values, so they would wrongly forbid the
	// repeats.
	# The derangements come in lexicographic order of the input positions they draw from.
	// reaching for: out sorted lexicographically by each derangement's sequence of source input
	// positions. `sorted_by` takes field keys, not a key that is a list of positions, and a
	// derangement's source positions are not recorded in it. Sorting by the item values would be
	// wrong whenever the iterable is not already sorted.
	// example wanted: ["Alice", "Bob", "Carol", "Dave"] with no r: the Secret Santa case the
	// docstring prints, order included
	// example wanted: ["0", "1", "2"] with r 2: a partial derangement compares each index of the
	// shorter tuple with the same index of the input
	// example wanted: ["Alice", "Bob", "Bob"] with no r: a "Bob" may sit at index 1 because it came
	// from index 2; identity is by position
	// example wanted: ["a", "a", "b", "b"] with no r: one value arrangement is drawn from several
	// position arrangements; the claim that each position arrangement appears once decides how many
	// times it appears
	// example wanted: the empty iterable with no r: whether the one empty arrangement counts as a
	// derangement (it has no index to fix)
	// example wanted: ["a", "b"] with r 0: the same question for the empty arrangement on a
	// non-empty iterable
	// example wanted: ["a", "b"] with r 3: no arrangement of three items can be drawn from two
	// occurrences
	// example wanted: ["a"] with no r: the only arrangement fixes its one element
	// example wanted: ["a", "b"] with r -1: Python's permutations raises here; the author decides
	// whether this becomes an error case in a Result
